# Performance

## Objective

Avoid clearly wasteful patterns (N+1 queries, unnecessary re-renders, quadratic loops on large data) without prematurely micro-optimizing code that doesn't need it.

## Core Principles

- Avoid N+1 database queries — batch or join instead.
- Avoid re-computing/re-fetching the same data repeatedly within a single request/render.
- Choose data structures appropriate to the access pattern (e.g. a Set for membership checks, not repeated array `.includes`).
- Don't sacrifice readability for a micro-optimization with no measured benefit.

## Requirements

- Batch database calls in loops instead of querying per iteration.
- Cache/memoize expensive computations that are reused within the same scope, not globally by default.
- Flag genuinely risky complexity (e.g. an O(n²) loop over user-controlled input) even without profiling data.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Querying the database inside a loop instead of batching.
- Adding caching/memoization for a rarely-called, cheap function 'just in case'.
- Rewriting clear code into a 'clever' optimized version with no measured performance need.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ No obvious N+1 or repeated-computation patterns were introduced.
✓ Data structure choices match their actual access pattern.
✓ No premature optimization sacrificed clarity without a measured reason.

## Golden Rule

Fix real, visible inefficiencies; don't guess-optimize code that isn't a proven bottleneck.
